[Music]  political corruption serves one not all  it subverts the very purpose of  government and creates distrust of  elected officials  corrupt politicians manipulate the  economy unfairly reward those loyal to  them and punish those who stand in their  way  hi i'm rich burnett for one dream and in  this episode of perspectives four  historians discuss political corruption  in the modern world and they explain how  two former corrupt presidents got  punished for their deeds  we begin now with a look at two basic  types of corruption bribery and  gratuities  [Music]  the quintessential public corruption  offense is bribery  bribery is generally defined as a public  official agreeing to accept something of  personal value  in exchange for the performance of some  official act or exercise of official  discretion  a bribery presents a fundamental threat  to the proper functioning of our  political system  the bribed public official has a  conflict of interest he or she is  supposed to act for the good of all but  instead is acting for some private  benefit and this is contrary to the very  nature of a representative democracy and  corrupts our political institutions  title 18 section 201 of the u.s code  contains two distinct but related crimes  bribery and gratuities  to prove bribery prosecutors must prove  the following  a federal public official  corruptly  demands seeks receives or accepts or  agrees to receive or accept anything of  value  in exchange for being influenced in the  performance of an official act  or being induced to do or omit to do  and act in violation of his or her  official duty  or being influenced to commit or aid in  committing any fraud against the united  states  bribery is punishable by up to 15 years  in prison  along with substantial fines and  disqualification from holding any office  of honor trust or profit in the united  states  to prove an illegal gratuity  prosecutors must prove a federal public  official knowingly  demands seeks receives or accepts or  agrees to receive or accept anything of  value  for or because of any official act  performed or to be performed  by the public official  an illegal gratuity is a relatively  minor felony as federal felonies go it's  punishable by only up to two years in  prison  now the statute also prohibits the flip  side of both offenses just described  it's illegal to pay a bribe or gratuity  as well as to receive it  gilded age americans were greatly  troubled by abundant evidence that big  business was corrupting american  democracy  some of the most infamous political  corruption scandals took place in this  era  for example the credit mobilier scandal  was exposed in 1872  it involved massively inflated contracts  and shady stock deals related to the  building of the transcontinental  railroad the scandal implicated two  prominent congressmen and many officials  in the grant administration  in another outrage of the time the  treasury department and liquor  distillers were caught colluding in the  infamous whiskey ring this was an effort  to defraud the federal government of  excise taxes  but many many more corruption scandals  percolated at the state level  for example the pennsylvania railroad  enjoyed astonishing power in influence  in his home state during the 1870s and  1880s  the railroad actually had its own office  in the state capitol building and its  chief lobbyist was known as the 51st  senator  and there's actually a story that on one  occasion after a day of passing several  bills favorable to the pennsylvania  railroad a legislator rose and suggested  that if mr tom scott who was the  railroad's president had no further  business for the legislature to attend  to it ought to adjourn  in new york state jay gould's erie  railroad and vanderbilt's new york  central railroad competed for the  influence of the state state legislature  in albany by distributing vast sums of  bribery money  the role of corporate money in politics  was neatly summarized by republican  power broker mark hannah who said in the  1890s quote there are two things that  are important in politics the first is  money and i can't remember what the  second one is unquote  big business seemed to enjoy at least as  much influence in the judicial system  from the local courts and judges all the  way to the supreme court  legal decisions bearing on business in  the late 19th century overwhelmingly  favored business over the public at  large  including especially industrial workers  and farmers  so by the time we are picking up this  story in the late 1960s filipinos had  endured two decades of tumultuous  independence under a series of  presidents who kept promising economic  recovery land reform to help poor  farmers and punish greedy landlords  and peace to a nation wracked by peasant  rebellion and a communist guerilla  movement  ferdinand marcos won the presidency in  1965 by promising to end the country's  economic woes  but his opponents stymied many of his  plans and crime and unrest only got  worse  his communist enemies got bolder and  formed the new people's army to promote  the armed struggle for revolution  while a group called the moro national  liberation front began fighting for  independence for the philippines muslim  minority  marcos responded by trying to stamp out  dissent and declaring martial law  but gradually the regime became alarmed  at the way catholic-based communities  were becoming a lasting political  network for the opposition  after 1982 marcos stepped up arrests of  catholic workers  all this time the filipino middle class  who were mostly catholic were getting  angrier and angrier at all the greed  corruption and brutality of marcos and  his cronies  his wife imelda became world famous for  her collection of hundreds of pairs of  designer shoes  the last straw came when marcos henchman  murdered the main leader of the reform  movement a former senator named benino  aquino as he was getting off a plane at  the manila airport  the middle class revolted and joined the  radicals and mass demonstrations known  as the people power revolution  on the night of june 17  1972  five burglars broke into the  headquarters of the democratic national  committee located in the watergate  complex adjacent to potomac river in  washington d.c  initially  the burglary seemed of of little  importance  it was characterized by president  richard nixon's spokesman as a  third-rate crime  but the fbi's investigation soon found a  connection between the burglars and  president nixon's committee for the  re-election of the president  as investigators later discovered this  was the second time the burglars had  entered the dnc offices the first time  in may they went in to take photographs  of campaign documents  and to install listening devices  foam wire taps to monitor political  calls  but the wiretaps had failed  and they needed fixing  the june operation was a black bag job  to repair them  even after the connection to the  president's re-election campaign came to  light  at first the watergate break-in  looked like it would pass nixon by  five months later in november 1972 he  was re-elected in a landslide  in january 1973 the five burglars along  with two other republican operatives g  gordon liddy and e howard hunt  were convicted of conspiracy and  burglary  that seemed to draw a line under the  problem  perhaps the end of the scandal was near  then it spun out of control  one of the burglars wrote the presiding  judge john cerrika and alleged that  there had been a high level cover-up by  more senior officials  the burglar mccord also alleged that  witnesses had perjured themselves and  that the burglars had been pressured to  maintain their silence  a short while later  jeb magruder the deputy director of the  president's reelection committee  admitted to investigators that he had in  fact perjured himself and he implicated  two top nixon confidants in the scheme  john dean the chief lawyer in the white  house  and john mitchell who had been nixon's  attorney general until he resigned in  1972 to direct the committee to re-elect  the president  during live testimony in front of the  senate former white house deputy chief  of staff alexander butterfield revealed  the existence of a taping system in the  oval office  the disclosure shocked the nation and  altered the course of american history  of course  cox demanded the tapes so did the senate  but nixon refused to release them citing  his executive privilege  by unanimous decision on july 24 1974  the supreme court ordered that nixon  turned the tapes over  released to the public on august 5th  1974 the smoking gun tape was of a  meeting on june 23 1972 just six days  after the break-in happened  it revealed that contrary to his  protestations president nixon had been  part of the cover-up of the burglary  almost from the very beginning  the topic of the meeting was how the  participants could stop the fbi from  conducting the investigation  nixon's aide h.r haldeman began the  conversation quote  the democratic break-in thing  we're back to the in the problem area  because the fbi is not under control  because fbi director l patrick gray  doesn't exactly know how to control them  and they have their investigation is now  leading into some productive areas and  it goes in some directions we don't want  it to go  holdemon told nixon that the burglars  had been financed by the re-election  campaign and then offered a plan for how  to contain the investigation  quote  the way to handle this now is for us to  have walters the deputy director of the  cia call pat gray and just say stay the  hell out of this this is our business  here we don't want you to go any further  on it  end quote  nixon agreed again quoting all right  fine i understand it all he said and so  he gave the fateful order quote you call  them in good good deal play it tough  that's the way they play it and that's  the way we're going to play it  the tape and its public revelation meant  the end of nixon's political support in  congress and across the country  earlier as we said several congressmen  had voted against the articles of  impeachment in the house judiciary  committee  now  many of them publicly declared that they  would vote for impeachment on the house  floor  on august 7th the republican leaders in  the house and senate paid an evening  visit to nixon  they told him that impeachment was a  certainty and that no more than 15  senators would vote to acquit him during  the trial  and so just a day later on august 8th  1974  nixon announced his intention to resign  [Music]  hey thanks for watching if you'd like to  learn more about the topics in this  episode the full list of series that  these clips came from is in the  description below you can watch them all  on one dream and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel for  new episodes of perspectives and you can  watch previous ones here  [Music]

